      "math_academy_id": "q-333267",
      "sequence_position": 5,
      "problem": "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?",
      "worked_solution": "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=5$ with the corresponding eigenvectors $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{5t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{5t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={e}^{5t}[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNotice that for each vector $[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n${e}^{5t}[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince ${e}^{5t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14124655/assets/2acd3b0de16138b460a332cf6e4841e6caeeb5dbb23eef8f411dbdb5ce48ea42.png)",
